\section*{Classification of Class-VII Complex Surfaces}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Every minimal class-VII compact complex surface with positive second Betti number is claimed to contain a global spherical shell. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Let $X$ be a complex surface that is compact and connected, satisfies $b_1(X)=1$ and $b_2(X)>0$, and has $\kappa(X)=-\infty$, and assume it has no rational curve that is smooth and has self-intersection $-1$. OpenAI claims a biholomorphism $\phi:U\to\Sigma\subset X$, where $U$ is an open neighborhood of $S^3=\{z\in\mathbb C^2:|z|=1\}$ in $\mathbb C^2\setminus\{0\}$, such that
\[
X\setminus\Sigma\text{ is connected}.
\]
This is a global spherical shell for each class-VII surface assumed minimal and satisfying $b_2>0$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 060, 2026-09-24): On an infinite cyclic cover, weighted Dolbeault and Fourier--Laplace estimates construct effective divisors in the necessary compact Chern classes. Residue currents, cylinder estimates, and cycle compactness make their components compact; the resulting rational-curve count invokes the established shell criteria.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 060, 2026-09-24): The global-shell theorem proves a precise structural assertion for every minimal class VII surface with positive b2, and its corollary fixes deformation and topology. The problem is explicitly a classification fragment with no defined equivalence or requested invariants; a solved classification label would require silently supplying that missing scope.

\subsection{Formal verification}
